<header><p class="enc-kicker">Work / monument · infrastructure</p><h1 class="enc-title">Bridgewater Canal</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1761">1761</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/18th-century">18th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Bridgewater Canal is a canal in north-west <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/england">England</a>, built for the Duke of Bridgewater to carry <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/coal">coal</a> from his mines at Worsley to Manchester. Engineered by James Brindley and John <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/humphrey-gilbert">Gilbert</a>, its first section opened in 1761 and halved the price of coal in Manchester. Its success set off a boom in canal building that supplied the early <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/industrial-revolution">Industrial Revolution</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/bridgewater-canal.jpg" alt="Bridgewater Canal" width="320" height="211" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Parrot of Doom · CC BY-SA 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Worsley_packet_house_closeup_large_image.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
